# Copy to .env.local for local development. Never commit real secrets -
# this file only documents which variables exist and what they're for.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Blog admin (/he/admin, /en/admin) - existing feature
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=
GITHUB_TOKEN=
GITHUB_OWNER=
GITHUB_REPO=
# Do NOT set GITHUB_BRANCH as a fixed value in Vercel's dashboard - it
# overrides the per-deployment branch fallback in lib/github.ts and was
# the root cause of a bug where every Preview deployment published posts
# to one fixed branch instead of its own. Leave unset; the code falls
# back to Vercel's own VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_REF (auto-injected, no action
# needed) or "main".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Time Tracking app (/app) - Phase 0/1
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres connection string. Local dev: run `npm run db:dev` (embedded
# Postgres on port 55432) and use the value below as-is. Preview/
# Production: the connection string Vercel's Postgres integration injects
# automatically once the database is provisioned and linked to this
# project - you do not need to copy/paste it by hand in that case.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://ankora:ankora_dev_only@127.0.0.1:55432/ankora_dev"

# The same database, reached WITHOUT the connection pooler. Prisma Migrate
# uses this one and nothing else does; see the comment on the datasource in
# prisma/schema.prisma for what goes wrong when migrations run through a
# pooler. Local dev has no pooler, so the value is identical to the line
# above. Preview and Production get it from Neon, already injected.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgresql://ankora:ankora_dev_only@127.0.0.1:55432/ankora_dev"

# Auth.js v5 session/JWT signing secret. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Required in every environment (local/preview/production) and must be
# set directly in Vercel's dashboard for Preview + Production - it is not
# provided automatically the way DATABASE_URL is.
AUTH_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting (security review follow-up) - OPTIONAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared, cross-instance rate-limit counters. Without these the app still
# rate-limits, but each serverless instance counts on its own, so a
# distributed attacker gets more attempts than the configured numbers
# suggest (see lib/rate-limit.ts). With them, every instance shares one
# counter.
#
# Vercel's Upstash Marketplace integration injects these automatically
# once an Upstash Redis store is linked to the project - nothing to copy
# by hand, same as DATABASE_URL. NOTE that it injects the legacy Vercel KV
# names (KV_REST_API_*), NOT the UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_* names Upstash's own
# docs use. The app accepts either pair, so both provisioning routes work;
# see lib/rate-limit-redis.ts.
#
# The app degrades gracefully: unset, slow or erroring Upstash falls back
# to the in-memory counters rather than blocking logins.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
# Alternative names, used when provisioning straight from Upstash:
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nightly backup -> Google Drive (Phase 11 follow-up) - see
# lib/google-drive.ts's doc comment for the full "why"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# NONE of the four values below is a secret. They are public identifiers.
# This app authenticates to Google with Workload Identity Federation, so
# there is no private key here to leak, rotate or expire - see
# docs/adr/0001 section 22.8 for why that replaced the original
# service-account-key design before it ever shipped.
#
# Setup (one-time, done by Ariel - Claude cannot create GCP resources):
#
#   1. Create (or reuse) a GCP project. Enable three APIs in it:
#      "Google Drive API", "Security Token Service API" and
#      "IAM Service Account Credentials API".
#   2. IAM & Admin -> Service Accounts -> Create service account (no
#      project role needed - Drive access comes from folder sharing,
#      not IAM). Do NOT create a key for it.
#   3. In Google Drive, share BOTH destination folders ("דוחות יומיים -
#      Excel" and "דאטהבייס - dumps") with that service account's email
#      (looks like ...@<project>.iam.gserviceaccount.com), permission:
#      Editor / Content manager. This, and only this, is what grants
#      Drive access. Keep those folders in a SHARED DRIVE rather than
#      someone's My Drive: a service account has no storage quota of its
#      own, so a My Drive parent can fail with storageQuotaExceeded no
#      matter how small the file, and files there would not survive that
#      person leaving. lib/google-drive.ts sends supportsAllDrives=true
#      accordingly.
#   4. In Vercel: Project Settings -> Security -> enable "Secure Backend
#      Access with OIDC Federation", Issuer Mode "Team". Note the issuer
#      (https://oidc.vercel.com/<team>) and audience
#      (https://vercel.com/<team>) it shows.
#   5. IAM & Admin -> Workload Identity Federation -> create a pool, then
#      an OIDC provider inside it pointing at that issuer URL, with that
#      audience allowed. Map google.subject = assertion.sub, and set an
#      attribute condition restricting it to this project's deployments,
#      e.g.
#        assertion.sub.startsWith("owner:<team>:project:<project>:")
#      Without that condition ANY Vercel deployment on the team could
#      federate in - it is the security boundary, not a nicety.
#   6. On the pool page, "Grant access" -> select the service account
#      above -> grant it to the principalSet for this pool. That is what
#      lets the federated identity impersonate the service account.
#   7. Set the four variables below in Vercel as Production env vars
#      (add Preview too if you want preview deployments to upload).
#
# Without these set, sendNightlyDataExport() still emails the report as
# before - the Drive upload step just reports an error (logged, not
# fatal) and the email remains the primary delivery path.
GCP_PROJECT_NUMBER=
GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_POOL_ID=
GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_POOL_PROVIDER_ID=
GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials vault (claude/credentials-vault-spec-2026-10-06.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clients' logins are encrypted per row; each row's data key is wrapped by
# a Cloud KMS key that never leaves Google. lib/vault/keys.ts REFUSES to
# run in production without KMS, so this setup is required before the
# vault can be used there.
#
# Setup (one-time, done by Ariel), reusing the Workload Identity pool
# above:
#
#   1. Enable the "Cloud Key Management Service (KMS) API".
#   2. Security -> Key Management -> create key ring "ankora-vault" in
#      location me-west1 (Tel Aviv). Inside it create key "credentials":
#      purpose Symmetric encrypt/decrypt, rotation every 365 days, and
#      the longest "scheduled for destruction" duration offered. Deleting
#      this key deletes every stored password with it.
#   3. Create a NEW service account "ankora-vault" (not the backup one).
#      Do NOT create a key for it. On the KMS key (not the project) grant
#      it "Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter".
#   4. On that service account -> Permissions -> grant
#      "Workload Identity User" to exactly ONE principal, production only:
#        principal://iam.googleapis.com/projects/<GCP_PROJECT_NUMBER>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/<POOL_ID>/subject/owner:<team>:project:<project>:environment:production
#      Not the principalSet the backup account uses. Neon preview branches
#      are copies of production, ciphertext included; a preview that could
#      reach this key could decrypt real passwords.
#   5. Only Ariel keeps "Cloud KMS Admin" on the key ring.
#   6. In Vercel, PRODUCTION ONLY:
#        GCP_VAULT_KMS_KEY = projects/<id>/locations/me-west1/keyRings/ankora-vault/cryptoKeys/credentials
#        GCP_VAULT_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL = ankora-vault@<id>.iam.gserviceaccount.com
#      In Vercel, PREVIEW ONLY, as type "sensitive":
#        VAULT_LOCAL_KEK = output of `openssl rand -base64 32`
#      Locally and in CI, any 32 random bytes in base64.
GCP_VAULT_KMS_KEY=
GCP_VAULT_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=
VAULT_LOCAL_KEK=
# Who is emailed about a step-up lockout or unusual reveal volume.
# Comma-separated. Defaults to ariel@ankora.co.il.
VAULT_ALERT_EMAIL=
